COMMAND "Add a bp CLI Verb" DESCRIPTION "Give a plugin a working `bp <noun> <verb>` by planting one data map at the head of its cli_commands/0 list. Variable-target (the add-plugin-route idiom): anchors on the `def cli_commands do` + `[` opener that add-plugin ships OPEN in every generated plugin module, so the verb composes on add-plugin's own output with zero hand-bridging. Zero Go: the manifest-driven CLI grows the verb for free." LAST_UPDATED "18-07-2026-00-00-00" DOMAIN "barkpark" TAGS "cli", "plugin", "verb" CONCEPT "cli-verb" VARIANT "read-list" DIRECTION "add" VARIABLES
  VARIABLE 1 "Plugin" TITLE "The target plugin (ships the cli_commands seam)" DESCRIPTION "Lowercase plugin name, referenced as {{.plugin}} in the target path api/lib/barkpark/plugins/{{.plugin}}.ex. Must be a plugin whose module opens `def cli_commands do` on `[` on two bare lines -- exactly what add-plugin plants OPEN in every generated plugin. A plugin that routes cli_commands through a different shape (tickets delegates to a sibling CLI module; sheets rebinds first) refuses loud at anchor resolution, and a typo'd plugin dies as the engine's typed PathMissingError (exit 4, nothing written)." EXAMPLES "bookmarks", "analytics"
  VARIABLE 2 "Noun" TITLE "Plugin noun the verb hangs off" DESCRIPTION "The noun `bp <noun> <verb>` answers to -- the lookup KEY the capabilities fold stamps with this plugin's provenance. Lowercase single word -- the lowercase casings coincide." EXAMPLES "bookmark", "ticket"
  VARIABLE 3 "Verb" TITLE "The new verb" DESCRIPTION "The verb `bp <noun> <verb>` will answer to. Lowercase single word -- the lowercase casings coincide." EXAMPLES "stats", "waiting", "unassigned"
  VARIABLE 4 "Summary" OPAQUE TITLE "One-line operator summary (manifest + --help text)" DESCRIPTION "Shown in `bp <noun> --help` and the capabilities manifest. OPAQUE: never re-cased, one spelling per file." EXAMPLES "Operator dashboard: open / answered / closed counts, oldest-waiting age."
  VARIABLE 5 "PathTemplate" OPAQUE TITLE "The HTTP route the verb calls (GET)" DESCRIPTION "The path_template the generic HTTP builder calls. Must be a route the API actually serves. OPAQUE: never re-cased and exempt from path-casing lints; this file spells it {{.path_template}}." EXAMPLES "/v1/bookmarks/stats"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Why this command is a ONE-MAP plant (the architecture already did the work)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is NO handler key to write. `bp` is manifest-driven: the Go binary
# (internal/cli) has zero per-command code. It reads GET /v1/capabilities, walks
# the noun/verb tree (tree.Lookup), and builds the HTTP call GENERICALLY from the
# command map's own fields -- `internal/cli/cli.go:9` composes the request from
# `http.path_template` via `manifest.BuildURL`, and `internal/cli/run.go:130`
# takes the method straight from `cmd.HTTP.Method`. No switch, no registration,
# no recompile.
#
# The round-trip, end to end:
#
#     cli_commands/0 list entry            (this plant -- the ONLY edit)
#        │  Plugins.<Plugin>.cli_commands/0 returns the list directly
#        ▼
#     GET /v1/capabilities                 (the capabilities fold stamps every
#        │                                   plugin map with provenance: the
#        │                                   OWNING PLUGIN's registered name,
#        │                                   looked up noun→plugin. The noun is
#        │                                   the lookup KEY, never the value:
#        │                                   capabilities.ex:200-213; doctrine
#        │                                   plugin.ex:506-508)
#        ▼
#     bp <noun> <verb>                     (tree.Lookup finds the map; the
#                                           generic HTTP builder calls
#                                           http.method + http.path_template)
#
# So: plant the map here, and `bp <noun> <verb>` works on the next capabilities
# fetch. No Go file is touched by this command -- that is the whole point.
#
# Exemplar payload: the `ticket.inbox` map, api/lib/barkpark/plugins/tickets/cli.ex:87-104
# -- the canonical read-list verb shape (id / noun / verb / summary /
# http{method,path_template} / auth_tier / args / flags / writes / batch /
# paginated / dry_run / default_output / scoped_prefix). This command templates
# that exact archetype: a read-tier GET list verb with a `limit` flag. For a
# WRITING verb, flip `method`/`writes`/`auth_tier`/`default_output` by hand -- the
# map SHAPE is identical, only these four values change.
#
# Optional cosmetic polish (NOT part of this command, never a MARK): if the new
# verb returns a single wrapped object and you want its fields rendered as
# key/value lines instead of a one-row table, add the envelope key to
# `singleObjectEnvelopeKeys` at internal/cli/table.go:96. That is a Go rendering
# nicety only; the verb is fully functional without it.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY HEAD-INSERT, NOT REANCHOR TAIL-APPEND -- the variable-target ruling
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This command targets a VARIABLE plugin, so it uses the add-plugin-route idiom,
# not the pinned-tail REANCHOR one:
#
#   * The old form REPLACE-pinned tickets/cli.ex, re-emitting the byte-exact
#     `ticket.close` tail map + comma + the new map. That anchor existed only
#     because the target was PINNED -- every other plugin's tail element is
#     different bytes, so a fenced tail-REPLACE is impossible across a variable
#     target.
#
#   * The byte-uniform anchor across every add-plugin-generated module is the
#     function head + list opener the skeleton ships OPEN -- grep-proven exactly
#     once per file, absent from the delegating/rebinding shapes:
#
#         def cli_commands do
#           [
#
# Verb ORDER in cli_commands/0 is NOT semantic (the capabilities fold indexes by
# noun+verb, tree.Lookup is a map), so head-insert is free: the anchor is never
# consumed (INSERT leaves it intact), so a second verb stacks directly under the
# opener above earlier plants -- newest-first, deterministic, no REANCHOR family
# needed. The trailing comma the payload carries always has the list's `]` (or an
# earlier planted map) to separate against, so the list stays valid whether it
# was born empty or already holds verbs.

# Token spellings-as-transforms (D3d / A1). Noun and verb are single lowercase
# words, so only the lowercase casings coincide (Pascal and SCREAMING differ) --
# declared for completeness:
#   {{.Verb}}   → Stats     (Pascal)
#   {{.verb}}   → stats     (kebab == camel == snake, lowercase)
#   {{.VERB}}   → STATS     (SCREAMING)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OP -- plant the verb map at the head of cli_commands/0 (A3)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Guard = the map's `id:` line, payload-derived (D3c): id IS verb identity, so a
# re-run skips clean AND a hand-authored duplicate verb refuses injection
# (stronger than guarding the mark line alone). The payload's comment line plants
# the per-verb mark cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}.
IN "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/{{.plugin}}.ex"
### Plant the {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} verb at the head of cli_commands/0
INSERT AFTER FIRST
::: cli_commands list opener :::
  def cli_commands do
    [
::: cli_commands list opener :::
WITH
::: {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} verb map :::
      # scaffy:add-cli-verb {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} MARK:cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}
      %{
        id: "{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}",
        noun: "{{.noun}}",
        verb: "{{.verb}}",
        summary: "{{.summary}}",
        http: %{method: "GET", path_template: "{{.path_template}}"},
        auth_tier: "read",
        args: [],
        flags: [
          %{name: "limit", type: "int", summary: "Max rows to return.", default: 50}
        ],
        writes: false,
        batch: false,
        paginated: true,
        dry_run: false,
        default_output: "table",
        scoped_prefix: nil
      },
::: {{.noun}}.{{.verb}} verb map :::
MARK "cli-verb-{{.noun}}-{{.verb}}"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "id: \"{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}\""

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTCONDITIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ASSERT FILE: the new map is present (id + route). Both strings are verbatim
# substrings the op writes, guard-consistent with the ASLONG above (A4).
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/{{.plugin}}.ex" CONTAINS "id: \"{{.noun}}.{{.verb}}\""
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/plugins/{{.plugin}}.ex" CONTAINS "path_template: \"{{.path_template}}\""

# ASSERT CMD: the world compiles with the new map wired. Every Elixir gate is
# TIER ci (A2) -- never run in the apply worktree, only on CI where the BEAM
# toolchain and DB are provisioned.
ASSERT CMD "cd api && mix compile" TIER ci
